 {"id":"fabro-81b7","title":"develop: implementer (and reviewer) must load and validate against the rust-style-guide skill","status":"open","type":"task","priority":2,"createdAt":"2026-09-06T19:04:15.238Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-06T19:04:15.238Z","description":"User review 2026-09-06: does the develop implementer use the rust-style-guide? Facts: (1) ACCESS EXISTS — run sandboxes discover the skill (agent.skills.discovered lists rust-style-guide from /workspace/fabro/.fabro/skills); (2) the implementer prompt NEVER mentions it (no occurrence of style.guide in .fabro/workflows/develop/prompts/implementer.md); neither does the reviewer prompt — the binding Rust policy is invisible to the whole develop pipeline. The 2026-08-27 fabro_run_logs lesson (agent-side): a skipped guide load shipped two logic bugs that a guide-first pass would have caught at design time; the local iterate skill got a mechanical gate for exactly this — the develop workflow needs the same. Wanted: (a) implementer prompt: BEFORE the first Rust edit, read the skill's guidelines.md index and the pages covering the diff (name the pages in the summary as proof-of-work); validate the implementation against them; deviations are findings, not accidents; (b) reviewer prompt: the standards axis IS the rust-style-guide — load workflows/code-review-refactor.md plus the pages covering the diff under review; (c) keep it cheap: pages-on-demand only, no full-guide reads. Basis: implementer.md/reviewer.md grep (zero mentions), sandbox skill discovery events (e.g. merge run 01M1V7XSV75B seq 25), iterate-skill mechanical-gate precedent, fabro_run_logs postmortem.","labels":["workflows","revision"]}
 {"id":"fabro-fb16","title":"engine: make Skip the safe default for scheduled automations + audit the untagged scheduler run path + surface on_overlap in the web UI","status":"open","type":"task","priority":2,"createdAt":"2026-09-06T19:09:33.140Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-06T21:42:19.600Z","description":"Follow-ups from the dd7e stacking incident (2026-09-06, fixed live by setting on_overlap=skip): (1) DEFAULT: on_overlap currently defaults to Fire — an unattended scheduled line stacks passes whenever nobody sets Skip (three parallel conductor passes while one ran). Make Skip the default for schedule triggers (or require explicit choice at creation) + test: scheduler tick with active tagged run creates NO second run. (2) AUDIT: stacked pass 01M1W1HHCE1M carried NO automation tag while sibling 01M1W1EZ5WZ did — find the creation path that drops it. (3) WEB — UPGRADED TO DATA-LOSS PREVENTION (user finding same evening): the automation edit dialog has NO on_overlap control AND its replaceAutomation payload omits the field entirely, so ANY save in the dialog silently resets a live on_overlap=skip back to default Fire (verified in automations-edit.tsx:107-115 vs ReplaceAutomation model). Wanted: an overlap toggle (Skip/Fire) in the form that round-trips the current value; visible posture next to the schedule badge on cards. Interim warning until landed: do not edit the conductor via the dialog.","labels":["engine","workflows","web"]}
 {"id":"fabro-ad23","title":"develop: evidence.nu dies printing large diffs — stdout write would-block kills the evidence stage","status":"open","type":"bug","priority":2,"createdAt":"2026-09-06T20:01:09.189Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-06T20:57:17.800Z","description":"Develop runs die in the evidence stage when evidence.nu prints large payloads: run 01M1VZTJSZ551NFPHPD429MG0 failed at scripts/evidence.nu:392 'print $diff' with 'I/O error: Writing to stdout failed: Operation would block' (nu print blocks when the piped stdout buffer fills with a 100KiB+ diff; script exit 1 -> soft 'Capture failed' exit, 67 min of work parked with no PR). STDOUT CAUSE FIXED in PR #32 (emit helper: line-aligned chunks under pipe capacity, pathological lines split at codepoints; smoke-tested with 200KB payload). REMAINING: (a) park reasons should carry the failed stage's output tail (exec_stdout_tail_bytes=0 — the diagnosis lived only in worker logs); (b) consider the same pipe-safe emission pattern for other stage scripts that print payloads.","labels":["workflows"]}
-{"id":"fabro-bc9b","title":"fabro-db: emit cargo rerun-if-changed for the migrations dir — sqlx::migrate! embeds silently stale in cached builds","status":"open","type":"bug","priority":1,"createdAt":"2026-09-06T21:19:49.947Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-06T21:19:49.947Z","description":"TWO instances on 2026-09-06: (1) local nextest: 'no column breaker_threshold' — the test binary linked a stale fabro-db rlib whose sqlx::migrate!-embedded MIGRATOR predated migrations/2026090601; cargo clean -p fabro-db fixed it. (2) PRODUCTION deploy #5 (just up, docker cargo-target volume cache): migrate() returned Ok but the binary's migrator lacked 2026090601 — /api/v1/automations 500'd while /health and fabro ps stayed green (caught only by the new smoke probe). Adding/removing files under lib/foundation/fabro-db/migrations/ does NOT reliably invalidate the fabro-db crate fingerprint in every cache context (local target + docker volume). Wanted: a build.rs in fabro-db emitting cargo:rerun-if-changed=migrations/ (and cargo:rerun-if-changed=build.rs) so ANY migration change forces recompilation of the crate and re-expansion of sqlx::migrate!; plus a defense-in-depth startup assertion comparing the embedded migration list against a version marker (e.g. log the newest embedded version at migrate() so logs prove which set ran).","labels":["engine","ci"]}
+{"id":"fabro-bc9b","title":"fabro-db: emit cargo rerun-if-changed for the migrations dir — sqlx::migrate! embeds silently stale in cached builds","status":"in_progress","type":"bug","priority":1,"createdAt":"2026-09-06T21:19:49.947Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-07T07:35:42.215Z","description":"TWO instances on 2026-09-06: (1) local nextest: 'no column breaker_threshold' — the test binary linked a stale fabro-db rlib whose sqlx::migrate!-embedded MIGRATOR predated migrations/2026090601; cargo clean -p fabro-db fixed it. (2) PRODUCTION deploy #5 (just up, docker cargo-target volume cache): migrate() returned Ok but the binary's migrator lacked 2026090601 — /api/v1/automations 500'd while /health and fabro ps stayed green (caught only by the new smoke probe). Adding/removing files under lib/foundation/fabro-db/migrations/ does NOT reliably invalidate the fabro-db crate fingerprint in every cache context (local target + docker volume). Wanted: a build.rs in fabro-db emitting cargo:rerun-if-changed=migrations/ (and cargo:rerun-if-changed=build.rs) so ANY migration change forces recompilation of the crate and re-expansion of sqlx::migrate!; plus a defense-in-depth startup assertion comparing the embedded migration list against a version marker (e.g. log the newest embedded version at migrate() so logs prove which set ran).","labels":["engine","ci"]}
 {"id":"fabro-0195","title":"engine: declarative automation definitions in the repo (config/automations/*.toml) with on_overlap + breaker_threshold as code","status":"open","type":"feature","priority":2,"createdAt":"2026-09-06T21:41:21.610Z","updatedAt":"2026-09-06T21:41:21.610Z","description":"User review 2026-09-06 ('warum ist der skip nicht in der workflow def gesetzt?'): the conductor automation exists ONLY as live DB state — hand-created via UI/API, on_overlap never set (default Fire caused the three-pass stacking night), breaker_threshold likewise unset. Nothing versioned declares the line's safety posture; a DB reset or re-created automation silently loses skip/threshold and re-exposes the stacking + breaker-less behavior. The legacy import path (import_legacy_directory_once, dir = <config>/automations) exists but the repo ships no files there and import is one-shot legacy-shaped. Wanted: declarative automation definitions (e.g. config/automations/conductor.toml: target, workflow, environment, schedule expression, on_overlap=skip, breaker_threshold) that are (a) reviewed in PRs, (b) reconciled on server start (upsert by id; live pause state PRESERVED — enabled flag stays operator-owned, like today's resume flow), (c) surfaced in the web UI as 'managed from repo'. The workflow.fabro itself cannot carry on_overlap (scheduler-scope, not graph-scope) — the seed is about the automation layer getting its own source of truth. Interim (applied live 2026-09-06): on_overlap=skip set via API on the conductor.","labels":["engine","workflows"]}
